  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000122622,
      "SummaryText": "Executive Summary:\nTo provide guidance on complying with the Environmental Protection Agency's (EPAs) Risk Management Program (RMP).  The program has many of the same elements that are covered in the Nevada's Division of Environmental Protection (NDEP's) Chemical Accident Prevention Program and OSHA's Process Safety Management (PSM) program. Nikkiso Cryo Incorporated (NCI) Cryogenic Pump Test Facility has hazardous toxic and flammable substances listed in the Code of Federal Regulations (CFR) at 40 CFR 68.130 in an amount above the \"threshold quantity\" specified for that substance, you are required to develop and implement a risk management program under a rule issued by the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA).  For the RMP program that is Methane (CAS 74-92-8) and Propane (CAS 74-98-6) are both listed with a threshold quantity of 10,000 pounds.  The program complies with the EPA's RMP Level 3 requirements. The RMP is submitted to the EPA using RMP*eSubmit online. The process is on a secure web-based system and allows for access to the RMP online, anytime. The EPA Facility ID# is: 100000170977.\nMany of the required elements covered in the NCI procedures EHS P-024-01 Process Safety Management (PSM) Program and EHS P-025-01 Chemical Accident Prevention Program (CAPP) are referenced in this procedure. NCI is certified to ISO 14001 Environmental Management Systems and ISO 45001 Occupational Health and Safety Management Systems.\nREFERENCES\n29 CFR 1910.119 Process Safety Management of Highly Hazardous Chemicals.\n40 CFR 68.130 Chemical Accident Prevention Provisions\n29 CFR 1910.1200 Hazard Communication\nNikkiso Cryo Inc. EHS-001 EHS Management Systems Manual (EHSMS)\nRelevant NCI procedures and Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) are noted throughout our EHSMS. NCI's original Chemical Accident Prevention Program (CAPP) Assessment Report was produced by PLT Associates in July 2000.\nTHE FACILITY AND THE REGULATED SUBSTANCES HANDLED\nThe facility is owned and operated b"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000122622,
      "SummaryText": "y Nikkiso Cryo, Inc. It is a cryogenic pump testing facility, using refrigerated liquefied natural gas (LNG) OR refrigerated liquefied petroleum gas (LPG, i.e refrigerated liquid propane or refrigerated liquid butane) as pump testing mediums. The on-site liquid is stored in two horizontal cylindrical tanks. The system consists of two cryogenic storage tanks for refrigerated testing fluid, four storage tanks for liquid nitrogen(LN2, used for cooling and purging, a non-regulated substance), two test pump vessels, a re-circulation vessel, two transfer pumps, associated piping, valves, heat exchangers, control system, etc. IT SHOULD BE NOTED that the facility can only have one regulated substance on site  AT ANY GIVEN SINGLE TIME, due to the fact that the facility has only one system set which can handle only one testing fluid at a time. The regulated substance handled is either methane, which is the major component of LNG, OR propane, OR butane. After the completion of each test the testing fluid is transported away from the facility, and the system is purged with liquid nitrogen (LN2) in preparation for the next test. \nNEVADA NDEP CAPP PROGRAMS AND OSHA's PSM PROGRAM\nThe RMP and PSM program in many aspects mirrors the requirements for the Nevada Division of Environmental Protection (NDEP) Chemical Accident Prevention Program (CAPP). CAPP applies to facilities that have select, highly hazardous substances in quantities above defined thresholds. These highly hazardous substances are distinguished from numerous other agency-regulated substances in that they will cause acute health impacts, that is, serious health impacts from a relatively short- term, low concentration exposure. Some of the highly hazardous substances are covered under CAPP solely due to their flammability; CAPP also applies in facilities that manufacture explosives for sale that are classified as division 1.1 through 1.5 in the Table of Hazardous Materials under 49CFR172.101.\nMuch like OSHA's PSM progra"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 2,
      "FacilityID": 1000122622,
      "SummaryText": "m the CAPP requirements fall into one of three categories; accident prevention, emergency response or public right-to-know. Through the accident prevention program, facilities are required to: evaluate and mitigate hazards, understand the design parameters of their processes and operate within the appropriate design limits, prepare comprehensive operating procedures, thoroughly train operators in those procedures and maintain the facility equipment and instruments to prevent premature failure. Through the emergency response program, facilities are required to develop an action plan for dealing with potential emergency situations and they are further required to coordinate emergency response activities with local responders, to ensure that the responders are prepared to deal with the emergencies appropriately.\nTHE FIVE-YEAR ACCIDENT HISTORY\nNCI has never had an incident involving LNG or LPG or the vapors from either that caused death, injury, property damage, environmental damage, evacuation, or sheltering in place."
    }
  ]
}